            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Birth of Intel</strong><br>\nIn 1968, Noyce and Gordon Moore left Fairchild to start their own company, Intel Corporation. Their vision was to continue advancing the technology of integrated circuits. Intel introduced the first commercially available microprocessor, the Intel 4004, in 1971. This innovation was instrumental in the development of computers, calculators, and countless other electronic devices.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Leadership and Management Style</strong><br>\nNoyce was not only an inventor but also a highly regarded leader. He valued a culture of openness and encouraged creativity among his employees. Noyce's management style was non-traditional; he preferred a flat organizational structure without the rigid hierarchy that was common in other corporations. This approach fostered innovation and teamwork at Intel.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Silicon Valley Boom</strong><br>\nUnder Noyce's leadership, Intel's success contributed significantly to the growth of Silicon Valley. The region became a hub for technology and entrepreneurship, attracting engineers and inventors inspired by Noyce's work. Silicon Valley's boom in the '70s and '80s is largely attributed to the semiconductor industry's expansion, which Intel was at the forefront of.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
