          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Personal Philosophy and Legacy</strong><br>\nNoyce believed in taking risks and the importance of failure in the pursuit of innovation. His philosophy was that an error is not a failure until one refuses to correct it. This mindset not only shaped Intel's culture but also inspired a generation of entrepreneurs and inventors who followed in his footsteps.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Awards and Honors</strong><br>\nThroughout his life, Noyce received numerous awards and honors for his contributions to technology and engineering. In 1978, he was awarded the National Medal of Technology by President Ronald Reagan. Noyce's work in microelectronics has been credited with profoundly impacting the global economy and the way people interact with technology.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Final Years and Impact</strong><br>\nRobert Noyce passed away on June 3, 1990, leaving behind a legacy as a visionary leader and inventor. His contributions to microelectronics continue to be a foundation for current and future technologies. Noyce's life's work laid the groundwork for the digital age and established the model for Silicon Valley startups that continue to influence the tech industry worldwide.</p>\n"
              },
